Backport of vLLM pull request #55506, "[Bugfix] Index mamba spec-decode block
tables by request slot, not batch row", by Karl0007
(https://github.com/vllm-project/vllm/pull/55506, head 5f71d6fa, still open on
2026-09-25). The root cause and fix direction were first published by
zebgop-ops (https://github.com/zebgop-ops/qwen38-flashnext-pp). Not our work:
the code below is the PR's, rebased onto vLLM 0.29.1rc1.dev47+gdc36fcce9 (one
docstring line differed). The PR's regression test is not included. Drop this
patch once the base image contains the PR.

Author of the code: Karl0007 <44745209+Karl0007@users.noreply.github.com>
Signed-off-by: Karl0007 <44745209+Karl0007@users.noreply.github.com>
(as on the PR's commits c99ba59e, a28e9022 and 5f71d6fa). The description and
the RTX 3090 measurements below are ours.

vllm/v1/worker/gpu/model_runner.py
vllm/v1/worker/gpu/model_states/mamba_hybrid.py
vllm/v1/worker/mamba_utils.py

Independent of the other patches; it changes vLLM's V2 model runner only.

With MTP, PP > 1, prefix caching and --mamba-cache-mode align, a request could
start repeating one token ("ductductduct...") and never recover, and the
engine could then stop with "CUDA error: an illegal memory access" on a middle
PP rank. The Mamba/GDN state copies that save and restore the recurrent state
at block boundaries read the per-step, batch-ordered block tables with the
batch row. A non-last PP rank runs that copy pp_size steps late, when the
batch-ordered tables already belong to a later batch, so the copy used the
counters of one request and the blocks of another: the first request's
boundary state was not saved and the other request's state was overwritten.
The copies now read the persistent per-request tables and index them by
request slot, as the V1 runner already does. Only the row index changes; the
copied bytes and kernel launches are the same.

On 3 x RTX 3090 (3x24GB+MTP, two requests of 6k-45k tokens at a time) the
unpatched image looped on the 12th request and stopped with the illegal memory
access 8 minutes in, during the 21st; with this patch 100 requests ran with no
loop or error, and the draft acceptance rate stayed at 80%.

